Perfect — let’s go for the **alternate beginner-friendly solution** to **1791. Find Center of Star Graph**, Boss.  
This version will help you understand how to solve it using a **degree counting approach** — works for any graph, not just a star.

---

## 🧠 Idea (Count the degree of each node)
In a **star graph**,  
- The **center node** is connected to **all other nodes**,  
- So its **degree = n - 1** (it appears in all edges).  

So, if we count how many times each node appears,  
→ The node with the **maximum count** (or degree) is the center.

---

## 🧩 C Code (Alternate Solution)

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int findCenter(int** edges, int edgesSize, int* edgesColSize) {
    // Since nodes are labeled from 1 to n,
    // n = edgesSize + 1
    int n = edgesSize + 1;

    // Create a degree count array
    int* degree = (int*)calloc(n + 1, sizeof(int)); // initialize all with 0

    // Count the connections (degree) for each node
    for (int i = 0; i < edgesSize; i++) {
        int u = edges[i][0];
        int v = edges[i][1];
        degree[u]++;
        degree[v]++;
    }

    // The node with degree == n - 1 is the center
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        if (degree[i] == n - 1) {
            free(degree);
            return i;
        }
    }

    // Should never reach here in a valid star graph
    free(degree);
    return -1;
}
```

---

## 🧩 Example
```
edges = [[1,2],[2,3],[4,2]]
```

| Node | Degree |
|------|---------|
| 1    | 1 |
| 2    | 3 |
| 3    | 1 |
| 4    | 1 |

✅ Node 2 has degree = 3 (which is n - 1),  
so it’s the **center**.

---

## ⚡ Complexity
- **Time:** O(n) → we loop through all edges once  
- **Space:** O(n) → for the degree array  

---

## 🧠 When to use this version
✅ If you’re learning general graph concepts  
✅ When the graph might not be a perfect star  
✅ Helps understand how degrees work in graph problems  

---

