---

## 🧠 Quick Recap

We’re cloning a **connected undirected graph**.

Each node is:
```c
struct Node {
    int val;
    int numNeighbors;
    struct Node** neighbors;
};
```

We’re given one node, and we must return a **deep copy** — a completely new graph.

---

## 🧩 Core Idea
We’ll use **graph traversal** (DFS or BFS) to:
1. Visit every node.
2. Clone it.
3. Map the **original node** → **new cloned node**.
4. Recreate all connections.

---

# 🥇 Solution 1: **DFS (Recursive)** — ⭐ Easiest and Most Beginner-Friendly

### 💡 Logic
- Use recursion.
- Whenever we visit a node:
  - If not cloned → create clone and store it in `visited[]`.
  - Then recursively clone its neighbors.

### ✅ Code
```c
#include <stdlib.h>

struct Node {
    int val;
    int numNeighbors;
    struct Node** neighbors;
};

struct Node* cloneDFS(struct Node* node, struct Node** visited) {
    if (node == NULL) return NULL;

    if (visited[node->val] != NULL)
        return visited[node->val]; // already cloned

    struct Node* clone = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    clone->val = node->val;
    clone->numNeighbors = node->numNeighbors;
    clone->neighbors = (struct Node**)malloc(sizeof(struct Node*) * node->numNeighbors);

    visited[node->val] = clone;

    for (int i = 0; i < node->numNeighbors; i++)
        clone->neighbors[i] = cloneDFS(node->neighbors[i], visited);

    return clone;
}

struct Node* cloneGraph(struct Node* s) {
    if (s == NULL) return NULL;

    struct Node* visited[101] = {NULL}; // since val ∈ [1,100]
    return cloneDFS(s, visited);
}
```

### 🧮 Complexity
- Time: **O(V + E)**
- Space: **O(V)** (recursion + visited array)

### 🧠 Why it’s great
✅ Super clear  
✅ Works perfectly for small graphs  
✅ Perfect for understanding recursion and mapping  

---

# 🥈 Solution 2: **DFS (Iterative)** — Using Stack

### 💡 Logic
- Same DFS idea, but we use our own stack instead of recursion.
- Good if you want to avoid deep recursion.

### ✅ Code
```c
#include <stdlib.h>

struct Node {
    int val;
    int numNeighbors;
    struct Node** neighbors;
};

struct Node* cloneGraph(struct Node* s) {
    if (s == NULL) return NULL;

    struct Node* visited[101] = {NULL};

    // Manual stack
    struct Node* stack[101];
    int top = -1;

    // Clone first node
    struct Node* cloneStart = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    cloneStart->val = s->val;
    cloneStart->numNeighbors = s->numNeighbors;
    cloneStart->neighbors = (struct Node**)malloc(sizeof(struct Node*) * s->numNeighbors);
    visited[s->val] = cloneStart;
    stack[++top] = s;

    // DFS Iterative
    while (top >= 0) {
        struct Node* cur = stack[top--];

        for (int i = 0; i < cur->numNeighbors; i++) {
            struct Node* neighbor = cur->neighbors[i];

            // If not cloned, create and push
            if (visited[neighbor->val] == NULL) {
                struct Node* newNode = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
                newNode->val = neighbor->val;
                newNode->numNeighbors = neighbor->numNeighbors;
                newNode->neighbors = (struct Node**)malloc(sizeof(struct Node*) * neighbor->numNeighbors);
                visited[neighbor->val] = newNode;
                stack[++top] = neighbor;
            }
        }
    }

    // Second pass: connect neighbors for all cloned nodes
    for (int i = 1; i <= 100; i++) {
        if (visited[i] != NULL) {
            struct Node* original = NULL;
            // find the original node (by value)
            if (i == s->val)
                original = s;
            else {
                for (int j = 0; j < s->numNeighbors && original == NULL; j++)
                    if (s->neighbors[j]->val == i) original = s->neighbors[j];
            }

            if (original)
                for (int j = 0; j < original->numNeighbors; j++)
                    visited[i]->neighbors[j] = visited[original->neighbors[j]->val];
        }
    }

    return cloneStart;
}
```

### 🧠 Why it’s useful
✅ Avoids recursion  
✅ Works for larger graphs without stack overflow  

(but the recursive one is cleaner)

